MF2 smoke: lock_bit=152 false=False lof=0 frames=7961 PASS

THE F WORD -- candidates on a 4-bit frame

  Longest run of consecutive frames in which a payload lane matches a
  candidate word.  A lane that matches for 32 frames is an acquisition
  the framer cannot tell from the real thing.

  candidate                                 CVSD speech  CVSD silence      constant 0     constant 1    random data  octet 1011001
  11111110  period 8, this spec                       0              0              0              0              8              0
  11111100  period 8                                  0              0              0              0              8              0
  11110000  period 8                                  0              0              0              0              8              0
  10101010  period 2 -- cannot pin an 8-state SF             88          24000              0              0              8              0
  11001100  period 4 -- cannot pin an 8-state SF              0              0              0              0              8              0

  Runs are in frames.  Acquisition is 32 (mf2/spec.md §4), so any
  candidate reaching 32 on a payload lane is a word a data card can
  imitate.  Period-4 and period-2 words are excluded structurally, not
  by this table: they cannot pin an 8-state superframe, so the octet
  counter and RX_OCT would have no phase (mf2/spec.md §6).

SUPERFRAME PHASE -- wrong phases admitted

  Every hook state x every bit alignment = 16 start-ups a payload.
  "false" is an acquisition at a wrong bit alignment; "held" is one
  still wrong when the clip ends.

  payload                                                F only        F + guard
  CVSD speech both channels                          0/16 false       0/16 false
  CVSD idle both channels                            0/16 false       0/16 false
  constant 0 both                                    0/16 false       0/16 false
  constant 1 both                                    0/16 false       0/16 false
  random data both                                   0/16 false       0/16 false
  the F word in C1, idle in C2                       4/16 false       0/16 false
  the F word in both                                12/16 false       0/16 false

  "F only" takes the guard out of the RECEIVER's check while the
  transmitter still sends it, which is what isolates the guard's
  contribution.  MF8 closed this at 0 wrong phases with F + guard
  (mf8-spec.md §3.3); the pass bar for MF2 is the same, 0 in the
  right-hand column.  These payloads are the benign ones -- the
  adversarial pair (F word against the guard pattern) is in `seats`,
  and it is not 0.

THE GUARD -- S at two states, and why 0,4 = 0,1

  The guard is a second, independent condition on a lane the F word
  does not occupy.  It must reject a period-2 idle lane AND a lane
  carrying the F word (mf2/spec.md §3.2).

  guard                                          F word in C1, idle       F word in both            idle both          speech both
  states 0,4 = 0,1   same parity, this spec                    0/16                 0/16                 0/16                 0/16
  states 0,4 = 1,0   same parity, swapped                      0/16                 0/16                 0/16                 0/16
  states 0,2 = 0,1   same parity, closer                       0/16                 0/16                 0/16                 0/16
  states 1,5 = 0,1   would displace A1, A2                     0/16                 0/16                 0/16                 0/16
  state 0 only        one bit, no parity pair                  4/16                 0/16                 0/16                 0/16

  False acquisitions per payload.  A guard that lets the F-word lane
  through has not done its job; the geometry rule is two states of the
  SAME parity carrying values different from each other and from F
  (mf8-spec.md §3.2, and mf2/spec.md §3.2 for L = 4).

S POSITION -- half a frame from F, not beside it

  At a wrong bit alignment the check reads two lanes SBIT apart.  A
  wrong alignment is a HOLE when both carry fixed content that
  satisfies the check: the framer acquires it and nothing releases it.
  Analytically, every byte a data card could carry, in every seat,
  under all 4 hook states, CVSD idle elsewhere:

  S at bit 1, beside F     [F][S][C1][C2]
    one data card, bytes that hold a wrong alignment:  seat 1: 0  seat 2: 1
      seat 2: 0xFB
    two data cards, the F word in one and every byte in the other:
      unsafe pairs  (1,2)

  S at bit 2, opposite F   [F][C1][S][C2]  this spec
    one data card, bytes that hold a wrong alignment:  seat 1: 0  seat 2: 0
    two data cards, the F word in one and every byte in the other:
      unsafe pairs  (1,2)

  On a 4-bit frame the unique opposite spacing is s = 2 (2s = 0 mod 4).
  At skew 2 the receiver reads F and S swapped onto each other, so the
  F word lands on itself and the guard fails.  Skews 1 and 3 read
  (C1, C2) -- the only payload pair -- which is the seating problem
  below.

SLOT RULES -- what may sit in the two seats

  1. Exhaustive and analytic: every byte, every seat, every hook
     state, CVSD idle elsewhere.

     one data card, bytes that hold:  seat 1: 0  seat 2: 0
     two data cards, the F word in one and every byte in the other:
       seats 1,2: holds, 520 (byte, hook state) combinations

  2. The framer, all 4 hook states x 4 skews (16 start-ups).

  layout                                                false         held
    seat 1 = F word, seat 2 = voice                      0/16         0/16
    seat 2 = F word, seat 1 = voice                      0/16         0/16
    seat 1 = F word, seat 2 = guard pattern              4/16         4/16
    seat 2 = F word, seat 1 = guard pattern              0/16         0/16
    both seats = F word                                  0/16         0/16
    two data cards, random content                       0/16         0/16
    one data card (0xB2), one voice                      0/16         0/16
    two voice cards                                      0/16         0/16

  3. The residual: the F word in one seat and VOICE in the other.
     The guard lands on voice, which satisfies it by chance, and the
     framer acquires when that happens 4 superframes running.  Speech
     at nominal line level (0.15 rms, as mf2_line.py).
     Over 30 s of speech that is once in 3077 attempts at that skew.

     tracking                                    acquired  released, frames      mean  never
     frame counter only                             24/24                 -         -     24
     frame counter + guard counter (this spec)      24/24            22-262   1.40 ms      0

  THE RULE.  MF8 forbids data cards three seats apart and has six
  seats, so the rule costs it almost nothing.  On MF2 the F-to-S
  spacing IS the only two channel seats: the forbidden pair is the
  entire payload.  There is no seat to move to, so TWO DATA CARDS ARE
  NOT A CONFIGURATION -- keying must enforce it mechanically, and the
  silkscreen must not reuse MF8's "seats 1-3" story (mf2/spec.md §5).

